const body = `<h1>4.2 — Enemy Animation: A Different State Machine</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    You wired the Player's animator in 1.6, so you already know how animators work: a parameter
    goes in, a state comes out, and your code never touches a frame of art. Today you meet the
    enemy's animator — the same API, the same method names, and <em>none</em> of your
    conventions. Its rules were decided by somebody else and written down nowhere except inside
    the controller itself. Reading those rules <em>before</em> you write a line is the whole
    lesson, and it is a skill that will save you in Unit 8.
</p>

${renderAskBanner('how do I read an animator somebody else set up?', 3)}

${renderDecompose('4.2', DECOMPOSE, 3)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">The Problem: A Statue That Slides</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Your enemy patrols, but it slides around as a single frozen frame. The art exists and the
        animation controller exists — they were simply never connected to any code. Your job is
        to drive it. But before you write a line, you must find out <em>what the animator wants</em>,
        and the only place that is written down is the animator itself.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>Step 1 — find the Animator.</strong> Select the MobileEnemy prefab and look at the
        child object. Which object carries the Animator component, and which carries the Sprite
        Renderer? <strong>Step 2 — open the Animator window</strong> and read the
        <em>Parameters</em> list. <strong>Step 3 — click the states</strong> and read every
        transition's conditions. Write down the exact names, types and numbers before you read
        any further.
    </p>

    <p>Here is what you should have found, next to what you built yourself in 1.6:</p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>&nbsp;</th><th>The Player's animator (you built this)</th><th>The enemy's animator (already in the project)</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Parameters</strong></td>
            <td><code>IsWalking</code> (Bool), <code>WalkDir</code> (Int), <code>Attack</code> (Trigger)</td>
            <td><code>Direction</code> (Int), <code>Attack</code> (Trigger)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>What the Int means</strong></td>
            <td><code>0</code> = Up, <code>1</code> = Horizontal, <code>2</code> = Down</td>
            <td><code>0</code> = Right, <code>1</code> = Up, <code>2</code> = Left, <code>3</code> = Down</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Where the Animator lives</strong></td>
            <td>A child object named <em>Sprite</em></td>
            <td>A child object named <em>Sprite</em></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Idle states</strong></td>
            <td>Yes. Your controller has idle states to fall back on when the player stops.</td>
            <td><strong>None at all.</strong> The controller holds only Walk and Attack states. There is nowhere to go when the enemy stops.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Facing left and right</strong></td>
            <td>One clip per direction, mirrored with <code>flipX</code> on the Sprite Renderer.</td>
            <td><strong>Two separate clips</strong> — WalkLeft and WalkRight. This controller never touches <code>flipX</code> anywhere.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Attack</strong></td>
            <td>A Trigger you set from your code.</td>
            <td>A Trigger you set from your code.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="callout info">
        <strong>The names look familiar. The numbers do not.</strong> Both machines are driven
        with <code>SetInteger</code>. In yours, <code>0</code> means Up; in the enemy's,
        <code>0</code> means Right. A line copied from one script into the other compiles happily
        and produces an enemy that walks sideways when it should walk up. Two rules follow from
        this, and they are the point of today:
        <ul>
            <li>Before you drive <em>any</em> animator, read its parameters and its transitions. Never infer them from the scripts you have already written.</li>
            <li>An Animator controller is a state machine with its own vocabulary. Two controllers that share an API share nothing else.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>The three parameter types, and why Attack is a Trigger.</strong>
        <ul>
            <li>A <strong>Bool</strong> is a switch. You turn it on and you are responsible for turning it off. Forget the second half and the machine is stuck in that state forever.</li>
            <li>An <strong>Int</strong> is a selector. It holds a value indefinitely, so whatever you set last is still true next frame — which is exactly why the enemy controller has no idle state: nothing ever tells it to stop.</li>
            <li>A <strong>Trigger</strong> is a one-shot event. It fires once and resets itself, so you cannot forget to clear it. Attacks are events, not conditions — which is why both animators use a Trigger for them.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Reading Someone Else's Machine</h3>
        <ol>
<li>Your player's <code>WalkDir</code> and the enemy's <code>Direction</code> are both Ints set by <code>SetInteger</code>. Before copying any animator line from one script to the other, what exactly must you check — and where do you check it?</li>
<li>The enemy's controller has no idle states. If your code simply stops calling <code>SetInteger</code> when the enemy stops walking, what does the player see on screen — and is that a bug in your code or a gap in the asset?</li>
<li>Your player mirrors one clip with <code>flipX</code>; the enemy uses two separate clips and never flips. What does the enemy's approach give you that yours cannot — and what does it cost?</li>
</ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Read the Docs: The API You Already Met</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        This is the level-3 substitute for a walkthrough: the API is laid out below and the code
        is not. You write the file. Every row here is a call you have made before — in
        <a href="1.6.html">1.6</a>, on an animator whose rules were yours — and the column that
        matters today is the last one. Nothing in this table is new; what is new is that the
        machine on the other end of it was configured by somebody who is not you.
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Signature</th><th>What you get</th><th>What it costs</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>void SetInteger(string name, int value)</code></td>
            <td>The setter you used in 1.6 for the player's <code>WalkDir</code>. Here the parameter is called <code>Direction</code>, and it has four values instead of three. Whatever you set stays set until you set it again.</td>
            <td>The name is a string, not a keyword: the controller must already have a parameter spelled exactly that way, and a near miss is a Console warning and a frozen sprite rather than a crash. The <em>value</em> is the harder half — <code>2</code> is not a direction, it is a number some transition compares against. A wrong value that is still a valid number moves the enemy into the wrong clip in complete silence.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>void SetBool(string name, bool value)</code></td>
            <td>The setter you used in 1.6 for <code>IsWalking</code> — a value that is true or false and stays that way.</td>
            <td>Two things. This controller declares no Bool parameter at all, so any name you invent here is a warning and nothing else. And a Bool you set is yours to clear: nothing resets it for you, which is exactly why your player's animation needed a line that set it back to false every frame.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>void SetFloat(string name, float value)</code></td>
            <td>The fourth member of the family, and the one you have never actually called. It was in 1.6's table for smooth values — a speed, or a point on a walk-to-run blend.</td>
            <td>It is the wrong tool for a direction. The enemy's controller declares <code>Direction</code> as an <strong>Int</strong>, and a setter whose type does not match the parameter is a warning and no change: the value never arrives, and nothing on screen tells you so.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>void SetTrigger(string name)</code></td>
            <td>The one-shot you fired from 1.6's mouse click. No value is passed, because being fired is the whole message, and the animator clears the trigger once a transition has consumed it.</td>
            <td>It is an event, so it may not be fired every frame — that is 1.6's <code>GetMouseButton</code> mistake in a new costume, where holding the button restarted the swing sixty times a second. And a trigger that no transition is listening for is cleared without doing anything at all, which looks exactly like code that never ran.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>T GetComponentInChildren&lt;T&gt;()</code></td>
            <td>The lookup from 1.6 that searches the object this script sits on <em>and everything beneath it</em>. It is the one that finds this enemy's Animator, because the Animator is on the child object named Sprite — the same discovery you made on the Player.</td>
            <td>It searches downwards and takes the first match it meets, so a second Animator anywhere in the children would win without a word. It is also a slower search than the plain version, which is why it belongs in <code>Start()</code> — once — and not in the method that runs every frame.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>T GetComponent&lt;T&gt;()</code></td>
            <td>The same lookup without the downward search: it looks only at the object this script is attached to.</td>
            <td>On the enemy root it finds nothing and hands you <code>null</code>, because the Animator is not on the root. That is 1.6's most-repeated bug, and it is nastier here than it looks: the null is <em>created</em> in <code>Start()</code> and <em>used</em> in the frame loop, so the error message points at the line that used it rather than the line that failed.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>float Mathf.Abs(float f)</code></td>
            <td>The size of a number with the sign thrown away — the comparison you used in 1.6 to work out which axis was being pushed harder, so that walking hard left would not lose to a gentle nudge upward.</td>
            <td>It throws the sign away, which is exactly what you want in order to <em>choose</em> a direction and exactly what you must not use in order to <em>name</em> one. The losing axis has to be kept raw: on the horizontal axis the sign is the entire difference between the value that means Right and the value that means Left.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>void Update()</code> and <code>void FixedUpdate()</code></td>
            <td><code>Update()</code> is the frame loop you have used since 1.4 — once per rendered frame, and where your player's animator has been driven since 1.6. <code>FixedUpdate()</code> runs once per physics step, in step with movement written onto a Rigidbody2D.</td>
            <td><code>FixedUpdate()</code> can run twice in one frame or not at all, so a parameter written there can be a rendered frame stale — visible as a half-step of lag on the frame the direction changes. Match the loop to whatever is actually moving the enemy: if the movement came from <code>transform</code>, then <code>Update()</code> is the loop that is in step with it, and the answer is not the same for every enemy you will write.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="callout info">
        <strong>Five facts the API table cannot carry.</strong> A signature tells you how to call
        something; it cannot tell you what the machine on the other end will do with it. These are
        the five that decide whether today works, and each one is asked about again below.
        <ol>
            <li>A one-shot is forgotten once it has fired; a switch you set yourself is yours to unset. That is the whole difference between a Trigger and a Bool, and it is why an attack may not be fired every frame.</li>
            <li>A number means nothing on its own. <code>0</code> is a direction only because a transition somewhere compares it to <code>0</code> — which is why the same digit means Up in your player's controller and Right in this one.</li>
            <li>A component that lives on a child object is reached with a lookup that searches downwards. The object your script sits on and the object that carries the component are not the same object.</li>
            <li>Some machines have no stopped state to return to. This controller holds Walk states and Attack states and nothing else, so an enemy that has stopped moving still has a clip playing.</li>
            <li>"Stay put" is usually not an instruction — it is the absence of a new one. If your code stops writing <code>Direction</code>, the animator keeps the last value you gave it and keeps animating it.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <p>
        Each question below opens with one of those five facts, then asks what it means for your
        enemy. Answer from the table and the callout above, not from the player's script.
    </p>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Predict Before Building</h3>
        <ol>
<li>Example 1 says a cue button is forgotten and a dimmer is not. Which of the enemy's two parameters behaves like the cue button, and which behaves like the dimmer?</li>
<li>Example 2 says the code <code>0</code> means something only against the board that defines it. If you paste a line that sets <code>Direction</code> to <code>2</code> expecting "Down", what will the enemy actually do? Check the table.</li>
<li>Example 3 reaches a component one level below. Why does the enemy root need the same trick, and what would happen instead of an error if a stray Animator existed on the root?</li>
</ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab: Drive the Enemy's Animator</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        There is no code in this section on purpose. Every call you need is a row in the table
        above, and every rule you have to obey is written in a window you can open. What is left
        is the deciding — and the specifications below name the object, the parameter and the
        question, and stop there.
    </p>

    <p>
        <strong>Step 1 — choose your approach.</strong> There is a complete, working script at
        <code>Assets/Scripts/Package Scripts/TopDown_EnemyAnimator.cs</code>. It is not attached
        to anything. Read it before you decide anything: it is short, it is commented, and it was
        written by somebody else — which is exactly the skill this lesson is about. Then choose:
        attach that script, or write your own driver inside <code>EnemyController.cs</code>.
        Either is defensible. Write down which you picked and why.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification: finding the machine.</strong>
        <ul>
            <li>Which object on the MobileEnemy prefab carries the Animator, and which one carries the Sprite Renderer? Check the Inspector rather than assuming it matches the Player.</li>
            <li>Which <code>GetComponent</code> family call reaches the Animator from the root — and what does the plain version hand back instead: a null, or the wrong component? Name the 1.6 bug you are re-creating.</li>
            <li>When should that lookup run: once at startup, or every frame? Which of the two did you do for the turret's target in <a href="3.1.html">3.1</a>, and why does the same reasoning apply here?</li>
            <li>If you chose the package script, which object must you attach it to for its own lookup to succeed? Read its <code>Start()</code> and say what it assumes about the object it sits on — and what it would find if you dropped it on the root instead.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification: reading the contract.</strong> This is the part that
        is new today, and it happens before a single line of your own.
        <ul>
            <li>Open the Animator window on the enemy's controller. How many parameters does it declare, what are their exact names, and what type is each one? Write them down character for character — you are going to type them into strings.</li>
            <li>Click the transition arrows one at a time. Which parameter does each arrow test, and against which value? You are collecting the mapping from integer to direction, and the controller is the only place it is written down.</li>
            <li>What does this controller have <em>no</em> state for? How can you answer that by looking at the state graph, without pressing Play at all?</li>
            <li>Which object carries the Animator, and which controller asset is assigned to it? Where does that asset live in the Project window?</li>
            <li>Which of your player's conventions does this controller not share — the meaning of the numbers, the number of values, the idle states, the mirroring? List every difference you found in the table above and check you have not missed one.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification: turning movement into a number.</strong>
        <ul>
            <li>Which quantity in your enemy's movement tells you which of the four values to write — the movement it was <em>asked</em> for, or the movement that actually happened? Say why, and say which one you can actually read in the method you are writing.</li>
            <li>The enemy can move on both axes at once, and one axis can be larger than the other. How do you decide which of the four values wins on a diagonal step? Compare the <em>sizes</em> of the two components with <code>Mathf.Abs</code>, the way you did in 1.6, rather than comparing their values — and say why that distinction matters when the enemy is moving left.</li>
            <li>Write the mapping out in words before you write it in code: which integer is Right, which is Left, which is Up, which is Down. Check every one of them against the Parameters list and the arrows rather than against your memory, and note where your player's numbers differ.</li>
            <li>Which call actually writes the value — and which member of the <code>Set</code> family matches <code>Direction</code>'s declared type? Read the type off the Parameters tab before you choose the setter, and say what the wrong one would do.</li>
            <li>What value does <code>Direction</code> hold before your code writes anything at all, and what does the sprite do while it sits there?</li>
            <li>What happens if you write a value that no transition tests? Is that an error you will see, or silence — and which is harder to debug?</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification: when the value gets written.</strong>
        <ul>
            <li>Which lifecycle method should write the parameter — <code>Update()</code>, which runs once per rendered frame, or <code>FixedUpdate()</code>, which runs once per physics step? What is moving your enemy, and which loop is in step with <em>that</em>?</li>
            <li>What does the package script use? Read it and find out rather than guessing, then say whether you would have made the same choice and what yours costs.</li>
            <li>There is no state in this controller for an enemy that has stopped. What do you want the player to see at the end of a beat, and can your code produce it — or is that a gap in the asset you would have to fix inside the controller?</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification: the attack.</strong>
        <ul>
            <li>Nothing in your enemy attacks yet, so what parameter is waiting for it — and what kind of parameter is it? Which call fires it, and what does that call take?</li>
            <li>How often may you set it with <code>SetTrigger</code>? Describe what happens on screen if you set it every frame, and name the 1.6 mistake you have just recreated.</li>
            <li>When <a href="4.3.html">4.3</a> and 4.4 add pursuit and contact damage, where in that flow will the trigger be set, and what will the Attack states need to line up with?</li>
            <li>Should the animation decide when damage happens, or should damage decide when the animation plays? Argue a position — this is a real architectural fork and Unit 8 will test it.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <p>
        Save, play, and watch a full beat. The enemy should face the way it walks, in all four
        directions, and stop animating only if you work out how to make it stop.
    </p>

</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->

<div class="checklist">
    <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Name the enemy's two animator parameters and their types, by reading the controller</li>
        <li>Recite the four <code>Direction</code> values without looking — and say how they differ from your player's</li>
        <li>Explain why the enemy's animator needs <code>GetComponentInChildren</code></li>
        <li>Explain what the enemy does visually when it stops, and why the controller has no answer for it</li>
        <li>State that this controller uses separate left and right clips and never uses <code>flipX</code></li>
        <li>Explain the difference between an animation asset existing and being wired into the controller</li>
        <li>Break this feature into parts small enough to look each one up on its own, and say in one line what each part does</li>
        <li>Say which earlier lesson each part of this feature came from, and where you would reread it</li>
        <li>Commit and push</li>
    </ul>
</div>

